{"meta": {"course": "cat100-supplement", "chapter": "05", "section": "03", "chapter_title": "AI-Enhanced Presentation Development", "title": "Image Generation for Explaining Ideas", "description": "CAT 100 textbook section connecting image generation for explaining ideas to AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."], "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development", "cat100_supplement_image_generation_for_explaining_ideas", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 3, "top_tier_hardened": false, "prereq_section_ids": ["cat100-supplement/05/02"], "practice_ids": ["cat100_supplement_5_3_image_generation_for_explaining_ideas_artifact_signal", "cat100_supplement_5_3_image_generation_for_explaining_ideas_annotation_signal", "cat100_supplement_5_3_image_generation_for_explaining_ideas_ai_boundary", "cat100_supplement_5_3_image_generation_for_explaining_ideas_calibration"]}, "content": "# Image Generation for Explaining Ideas\n\n![Image Generation for Explaining Ideas textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/05-03-image-generation-for-explaining-ideas.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nAI image generators make it trivial to produce a picture, which is exactly why they are dangerous on a slide. A generated image looks authoritative, but the model does not know your process; it predicts plausible-looking pixels. If you ask for a diagram of your data pipeline, the tool may confidently produce one with the stages in the wrong order, and a viewer will trust the picture precisely because it looks finished.\n\nThis section teaches the difference between an image that explains and an image that decorates, and the verification habit that keeps a generated diagram honest. The artifact is an AI critique log recording which generated outputs you accepted, modified, and rejected, so a reviewer can see that you checked the picture against reality rather than pasting whatever the model produced.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student needs a slide that explains how their app moves data: collect, then clean, then analyze. They type \"make a cool tech graphic for data\" into an image tool and get a glowing brain wired to floating numbers. It looks impressive and explains nothing; a viewer learns the slide is \"about data\" and stops there. The student who instead prompts for three labeled boxes in a specific order, then checks each label against the real steps, gets a picture that does the teaching the bullets would otherwise have to do.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nAn image on a slide is either **explanatory** or **decorative**, and you should know which one you are placing. An explanatory image carries information the audience needs and that text alone would carry less efficiently: a process flow, a relationship, a before-and-after, a labeled structure. A decorative image carries mood but no information; remove it and the slide loses nothing it needed. Decoration is not banned, but it must never crowd out or impersonate explanation.\n\nGenerated images add a second axis: correctness. The model produces something that *looks* like an explanation without any guarantee that the content is true. So an explanatory generated image must pass two tests, in order:\n\n- **Specification.** Did you tell the model the exact parts, labels, and relationships, rather than asking for a vibe? \"Three labeled boxes, Collect then Clean then Analyze, arrows showing flow\" is a specification; \"a cool data graphic\" is a vibe.\n- **Verification.** Does every label, arrow, count, and order in the output match your real process? Generators routinely transpose steps, misspell labels, and invent extra elements.\n\nThe data-ink anchor decides whether the image earns its place (does it carry meaning the slide needs); the verification step decides whether the meaning it carries is true. An unverified explanatory image is worse than no image, because it teaches the audience something false with the full authority of a finished picture.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nGoal: generate an image that explains a three-stage data pipeline rather than decorates the slide.\n\n1. Write a precise prompt that names the parts and their relationship:\n\n   ```\n   Flat diagram, three labeled boxes left to right:\n   \"Collect\" -> \"Clean\" -> \"Analyze\", connected by arrows\n   showing data flow. No people, no robots, white background.\n   ```\n\n2. Generate and inspect the result against the real process. First output reversed two stages:\n\n   `Collect -> Analyze -> Clean`  (wrong: analysis cannot precede cleaning).\n\n3. Revise the prompt to lock the order, then regenerate:\n\n   `... in this exact order: Collect, then Clean, then Analyze ...`\n\n4. Verify every label and arrow matches your actual steps before placing the image.\n\nThe reversed-order output is the teaching moment. The picture looked clean and professional and was still wrong, which is exactly how a generated diagram fails. Your critique log should record that you rejected the first output for transposing two stages, modified the prompt to fix it, and accepted the second only after checking every arrow against your real pipeline.\n\nRun the specify-then-verify loop yourself, predicting each move before you reveal it:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Generate a pipeline diagram that explains, not decorates\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"You need a diagram of your three-stage pipeline. What kind of prompt do you write first?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"\\\"Make a cool tech graphic for data\\\"\",\"Three labeled boxes, Collect then Clean then Analyze, arrows showing flow, no people\",\"A glowing brain wired to floating numbers\",\"\\\"Something modern and professional about data\\\"\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"You write a specification: exact parts, exact labels, exact relationship. A vibe prompt produces decoration; a specification produces an explanation you can check.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The first output reads Collect then Analyze then Clean. Before placing it, what do you do?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Place it; it looks clean and professional\",\"Reject it and re-prompt to lock the order, because analysis cannot precede cleaning\",\"Keep it but shrink it so the error is less visible\",\"Add a caption apologizing for the order\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Reject and re-prompt. The polished look does not make the order correct. You lock the sequence: \\\"in this exact order: Collect, then Clean, then Analyze.\\\"\"},{\"reveal\":\"Verify the regenerated image: every label, arrow, and order must match your real pipeline before it goes on the slide. Only then is it accepted, and your log records the rejection and the check.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A generated diagram looks polished and professional. Why is that not evidence the content is correct?\" explanation=\"The model optimizes for plausible appearance, not for matching your data. Visual polish and factual correctness are unrelated in generated images, which is why verification against your real process is mandatory.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Polish guarantees the labels and order are right\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Polish and correctness are unrelated; the model optimizes appearance, not truth\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"A polished image never needs a caption\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Professional styling means the tool checked the facts\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nDecide whether the image explains or decorates, specify generated explanations precisely, and verify every element against reality before it goes on the slide. A polished image you did not check is a liability, not an asset.\n\nRun a generated image through three questions before you use it:\n\n1. **Purpose check:** Does this image carry information the audience needs, or is it decoration standing in for content?\n2. **Evidence check:** Does every label, count, order, and arrow match your real process or data, and where did you confirm it?\n3. **Revision check:** Which generated output did you reject or re-prompt, and what was wrong with it?\n\nIf you cannot point to where you verified the image, treat it as unverified and do not present it as an explanation.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is \"the image looks professional, so it must be right.\" Visual polish and factual correctness are unrelated in generated images; the model optimizes for plausible appearance, not for matching your data.\n\nThe second is writing about the tool (\"I used AI to make a diagram\") instead of showing what you checked. The grade-worthy move is not that you generated an image; it is that you caught the reversed stages and fixed them.\n\nOrder the specify-then-verify loop so each step depends on the one before it:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Decide whether the slide needs an explanatory image or only decoration\", \"Write a specification that names the exact parts, labels, and order\", \"Generate the image from the specification\", \"Check every label, arrow, and order against your real process\", \"Re-prompt to fix any element that does not match reality\", \"Place the image only after it passes verification, and log the rejection\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the two tests to a different content type: a generated icon set meant to label four product features. Specification first: rather than \"icons for my app,\" you name each feature and the concept its icon must signal, so \"upload\" reads as an arrow into a tray, not a generic cloud. Verification second: you check that the four icons are visually distinct and that none accidentally implies the wrong feature, because a \"download\" arrow where you meant \"upload\" silently misleads.\n\nThen weigh explanation against decoration. If the four icons help the audience tell the features apart at a glance, they carry meaning and stay. If they are just pretty thumbnails next to text that already names each feature, they are decoration competing for attention, and the honest move is to drop them. The same discipline that fixed the pipeline order applies here: specify, verify, and keep only what carries meaning.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: on the left, each generated image you considered; on the right, whether it explains or decorates and what you verified. Flag any image you placed without checking its content. By the end of 05.03, your note should show a reviewer that no generated picture went on a slide unverified.\n\nA strong reader of this section can answer four questions:\n\n1. Does this image explain something the audience needs, or just decorate?\n2. What exact parts and relationships did the prompt specify?\n3. What in the output did you check against reality, and what failed?\n4. Which output did you reject or re-prompt, and why?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe critique log is strong when a reviewer can see, for each generated image, the prompt, the flaw you caught, the fix, and the final verification. Treat the polished final image as the least interesting part of the record. The decisions that matter are the rejection and the check.\n\nA useful entry would sound like this: \"I rejected the first pipeline diagram because it ordered the stages Collect, Analyze, Clean, which is impossible since cleaning precedes analysis. I re-prompted to lock the order and accepted the second only after confirming all three labels and both arrows matched my real steps. I rejected a third, flashier version with a glowing background because the glow carried no meaning and reduced contrast on the labels.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nSuppose the assignment bans generated images entirely and you must explain the same pipeline with a built-in shape tool. The explain-or-decorate test and the verification habit transfer cleanly: you still draw three labeled boxes in the correct order, you still check the labels against your real steps, and you still drop any element that does not carry meaning. The tool changed; the reasoning that makes the image honest did not.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nConnect the ideas this section builds on. See how specifying and verifying a generated image feeds evidence-based revision of the critique log:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_image_generation_for_explaining_ideas\", \"cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/05-03-image-generation-for-explaining-ideas-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"05.03\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation of explanatory versus decorative images tied to data-ink reasoning.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a verified pipeline diagram with an unverified one that has stages out of order.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the specify-then-verify habit to a different generated graphic.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where verifying an image caught a factual error.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions for CAT 100 05.03. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_image_generation_for_explaining_ideas\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm each generated image is labeled explanatory or decorative, every explanatory image has been verified against your real content, and your log records at least one output you rejected and why.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_5_3_image_generation_for_explaining_ideas_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_image_generation_for_explaining_ideas", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_image_generation_for_explaining_ideas", "cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development"], "stem": "You need an image that explains a three-stage data pipeline (collect, clean, analyze). Which choice explains the idea rather than decorates the slide?", "options": ["A glossy AI image of a humanoid robot holding a tablet.", "A generated diagram with three labeled boxes 'Collect -> Clean -> Analyze' and an arrow showing data flow, checked against your real steps.", "A stock photo of people in a meeting room.", "An abstract swirl of blue lines that looks technical."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "The labeled three-stage diagram carries the meaning; removing it would lose the explanation, so it is explanatory not decorative.", "difficulty": "easy", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_5_3_image_generation_for_explaining_ideas_decorative_image"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_5_3_image_generation_for_explaining_ideas_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "evidence_based_revision", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision"], "stem": "Which note tells BigAL exactly what to help you fix in your generated image?", "options": ["This picture is cool.", "The generated pipeline diagram puts 'Analyze' before 'Clean', which reverses my real process. How do I prompt for the correct order?", "I need a better image.", "Make it prettier."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "The note names a specific accuracy fault (reversed stages) so feedback can target the prompt revision.", "difficulty": "medium", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_5_3_image_generation_for_explaining_ideas_vague_prompt"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_5_3_image_generation_for_explaining_ideas_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ai_supported_learning", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning"], "stem": "What is a sound boundary for using AI image generation here?", "options": ["Generate any image and trust its labels as accurate.", "Generate a draft diagram, then verify each label and arrow against your real pipeline before using it.", "Generate ten images and pick the prettiest regardless of meaning.", "Avoid generated images entirely even when a diagram would explain the idea."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "The generated diagram is a draft you must verify against the real artifact before it counts as an explanation.", "difficulty": "hard", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_5_3_image_generation_for_explaining_ideas_trust_generated_facts"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_5_3_image_generation_for_explaining_ideas_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "evidence_based_revision", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision"], "stem": "Your first prompt gave a vague image. Which revised prompt is most likely to explain the concept?", "options": ["'Make a nice picture about computers.'", "'Diagram three labeled boxes Collect, Clean, Analyze connected left to right by arrows, flat style, no people.'", "'Something tech and modern.'", "'A cool robot.'"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A precise prompt naming the parts, labels, and relationship is far more likely to produce an explanatory image.", "difficulty": "medium"}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "6dcf2bed0593da040dc040360d2625cea765f1040991679e71454b21daf1c753", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_5_3_image_generation_for_explaining_ideas_decorative_image", "pattern": "image_as_decoration", "description": "Generating images that decorate the slide instead of explaining the idea", "trigger": "learner adds an AI image of a generic robot to a slide about data pipelines", "feedback": "Ask what the image must teach; if removing it loses no meaning, it is decoration, not explanation.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_5_3_image_generation_for_explaining_ideas_vague_prompt", "pattern": "vague_generation_prompt", "description": "Using a vague image prompt and accepting whatever the model returns", "trigger": "learner prompts 'make a nice tech picture' and uses the first result unchanged", "feedback": "Write a prompt that names the concept, the parts to show, and the relationship; iterate until the image matches the idea.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_5_3_image_generation_for_explaining_ideas_trust_generated_facts", "pattern": "generated_image_as_evidence", "description": "Treating an AI-generated diagram as factually accurate without checking it", "trigger": "learner uses a generated flowchart whose arrows contradict the real process", "feedback": "Verify every label and arrow against the real artifact; a generated diagram is a draft, not a source of truth.", "rail_action": "explain"}]}